from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "6. Healing in Acts 9 to 28"),
("p", "Claim: the book of Acts records healings through Peter and through Paul in the chapters after Paul’s call, in the period the volume calls the overlap. The last healing the record gives is on Malta, in the last chapter, and the record closes with two years in which Paul preaches and no healing is recorded. The chapter reports each healing as the text reports it and does not assign the events to a single program."),
("sub", "6.1 The period"),
("p", "Acts 9 to 28 is the overlap. In these chapters Israel’s Prophecy Program was still operative and the Mystery Program had begun. The healings of the period are recorded in settings shaped by Israel’s Prophecy Program, which remained operative throughout Acts 9 to 28 even as the Mystery Program began. Where Paul or his companions are the ones working signs, the volume reports the text and does not assign the event to a single program. A healing that Paul works does not become Prophecy Program content because it occurs in such a setting, and it does not become content of the Mystery. Volume 1 reads the signs of these chapters as a whole in chapter 8, and this chapter takes only the healings."),
("sub", "6.2 Peter at Lydda and Joppa"),
("p", "The chapter that records Paul’s call also records two healings by Peter. At Lydda Peter found “a certain man named Æneas, which had kept his bed eight years, and was sick of the palsy,” and said to him, “Æneas, Jesus Christ maketh thee whole: arise, and make thy bed. And he arose immediately” (Acts 9:33, 34). “And all that dwelt at Lydda and Saron saw him, and turned to the Lord” (Acts 9:35). At Joppa a disciple named Tabitha “was sick, and died,” and Peter “kneeled down, and prayed; and turning him to the body said, Tabitha, arise. And she opened her eyes: and when she saw Peter, she sat up” (Acts 9:37, 40). He “presented her alive” to the saints and widows (Acts 9:41)."),
("p", "The two healings are reported as the text reports them. They show Peter working as he worked in Acts 3 and 5, with the name of Jesus Christ named at Lydda and with prayer at Joppa. The record does not say that the healing was given to any believer of the assemblies Paul founded."),
("sub", "6.3 Paul at Iconium and Lystra"),
("p", "At Iconium Paul and Barnabas went “into the synagogue of the Jews” and “abode they speaking boldly in the Lord, which gave testimony unto the word of his grace, and granted signs and wonders to be done by their hands” (Acts 14:1, 3). At Lystra “there sat a certain man at Lystra, impotent in his feet, being a cripple from his mother’s womb, who never had walked” (Acts 14:8). “The same heard Paul speak: who stedfastly beholding him, and perceiving that he had faith to be healed, Said with a loud voice, Stand upright on thy feet. And he leaped and walked” (Acts 14:9, 10)."),
("p", "The crowd’s answer is recorded: “The gods are come down to us in the likeness of men,” and the priest of Jupiter “brought oxen and garlands unto the gates, and would have done sacrifice with the people” (Acts 14:11, 13). Barnabas and Paul “rent their clothes” and cried, “We also are men of like passions with you, and preach unto you that ye should turn from these vanities unto the living God” (Acts 14:14, 15). The healing is followed at once by a refusal of the credit. A few verses later the same Paul is stoned: certain Jews “having stoned Paul, drew him out of the city, supposing he had been dead. Howbeit, as the disciples stood round about him, he rose up, and came into the city” (Acts 14:19, 20). The record gives no word on how he rose."),
("sub", "6.4 Paul at Ephesus"),
("p", "At Ephesus Paul spoke in the synagogue for three months and then taught in the school of Tyrannus for two years, “so that all they which dwelt in Asia heard the word of the Lord Jesus, both Jews and Greeks” (Acts 19:8–10). Luke then writes: “And God wrought special miracles by the hands of Paul: So that from his body were brought unto the sick handkerchiefs or aprons, and the diseases departed from them, and the evil spirits went out of them” (Acts 19:11, 12). The sentence names God as the one who worked, Paul’s hands as the means, and the handkerchiefs and aprons as the way the healing reached the sick. It calls the miracles “special,” a word the volume reports and does not define."),
("sub", "6.5 Eutychus at Troas"),
("p", "At Troas “a certain young man named Eutychus, being fallen into a deep sleep: and as Paul was long preaching, he sunk down with sleep, and fell down from the third loft, and was taken up dead” (Acts 20:9). Paul “fell on him, and embracing him said, Trouble not yourselves; for his life is in him” (Acts 20:10). They “brought the young man alive, and were not a little comforted” (Acts 20:12). The record reports an event in an assembly of disciples that met on the first day of the week to break bread (Acts 20:7). The event is a restoration of a young man to life. The text does not describe it as a gift or as a practice, and it records it once."),
("sub", "6.6 Malta, and the close of the record"),
("p", "The last healing the book records is on Malta. “And it came to pass, that the father of Publius lay sick of a fever and of a bloody flux: to whom Paul entered in, and prayed, and laid his hands on him, and healed him. So when this was done, others also, which had diseases in the island, came, and were healed” (Acts 28:8, 9). The sentence names the sick man, the means, which are prayer and the laying on of hands, and a result that spreads to the island. The honours that follow are recorded: “Who also honoured us with many honours; and when we departed, they laded us with such things as were necessary” (Acts 28:10)."),
("p", "The book then closes. “And Paul dwelt two whole years in his own hired house, and received all that came in unto him, Preaching the kingdom of God, and teaching those things which concern the Lord Jesus Christ, with all confidence, no man forbidding him” (Acts 28:30, 31). The two years record preaching and teaching. No healing is recorded in them, and none is recorded after Malta. The volume reports that observation and builds the argument of chapter 12 on it together with the sentence of Acts 28:25 to 28, which Volume 1 and Acts Commentary Volume 5 read."),
("sub", "6.7 What the record leaves unsaid"),
("p", "The record leaves some matters unsaid. It does not say why Paul’s healings were done at Lystra, Ephesus and Malta and not elsewhere. It does not say that any healing was done in the regular life of the assemblies Paul founded. It does not record a healing of Epaphroditus, of Timothy or of Trophimus, whose sicknesses the letters mention. The volume records these silences and returns to them in chapter 9."),
("tbl", "Healing in Acts 9 to 28.", [
    ("Acts 9:34", T("Acts 9:34"), "Peter at Lydda: Æneas is made whole in the name of Jesus Christ."),
    ("Acts 14:9, 10", T("Acts 14:9, 10"), "Paul at Lystra: a man who never had walked leaps and walks."),
    ("Acts 19:11, 12", T("Acts 19:11, 12"), "God wrought special miracles by Paul’s hands at Ephesus."),
    ("Acts 28:8", T("Acts 28:8"), "On Malta Paul prays, lays his hands on the sick and heals him."),
    ("Acts 28:30, 31", T("Acts 28:30, 31"), "Two years of preaching follow; no healing is recorded."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Acts 9 to 28 records healings by Peter and by Paul, in the overlap, reported as written and not assigned to a single program.",
    "Paul refuses the credit for the healing at Lystra and names the living God.",
    "The last healing the book records is on Malta, and none is recorded in the two years that follow.",
    "The record gives no healing in the regular life of the assemblies and none for the sick fellow workers of the letters.",
  ]),
]
